1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,280
This course takes you from nothing installed to a working feed.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:11,720
You install smeltr, bring in real logs, shape them, deliver them, and fix what breaks.

3
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:16,130
First, the problem smeltr solves, and how it's put together.

4
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:18,300
Logs are made everywhere.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:24,110
Servers, firewalls, applications and cloud services each write their own format.

6
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:26,620
They're used somewhere else.

7
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,920
A search tool, a security tool, an archive.

8
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:37,020
Connect each source directly to each tool, and every tool parses every format itself.

9
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:41,250
Every tool needs its own parser for every format.

10
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:48,400
When a format changes, every one of those parsers breaks, and you fix each one on its own.

11
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:53,300
A dozen parsers, each written by someone who has since moved on.

12
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,740
Even with only one tool, there's a second cost.

13
00:00:56,740 --> 00:01:00,180
Many tools charge by how much you send them each day.

14
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:03,950
Every line counts, whether anyone ever searches it or not.

15
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:05,810
There's a better way.

16
00:01:05,810 --> 00:01:08,070
Put a pipeline layer in between.

17
00:01:08,070 --> 00:01:12,040
Every source sends to it, and it sends on to every tool.

18
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:16,120
A format is parsed once, in one place.

19
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:21,610
When a format changes, you fix it once, and every tool gets the fix.

20
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:24,440
And you choose what each tool gets.

21
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:26,820
The search tool gets every login.

22
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:29,710
The archive gets everything, compressed.

23
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:31,190
And you send less.

24
00:01:31,190 --> 00:01:35,450
Drop the lines nobody searches, and remove the fields nobody reads,

25
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:38,510
before they reach a tool that charges by volume.

26
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:42,460
You pay for what you send, and now you send only what you use.

27
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:45,450
That's the first practice of this course.

28
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:51,210
Keep the shaping of your data in one place, between where it's made and where it's used.

29
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,420
In smeltr, that layer has three parts.

30
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:57,540
A smelter turns raw ore into metal.

31
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:00,320
smeltr turns raw logs into records.

32
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:03,820
We'll let you decide which is more precious.

33
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:06,870
A source is where records come from.

34
00:02:06,870 --> 00:02:11,310
A port that receives syslog, a Kafka topic, a cloud bucket.

35
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:15,960
A pipeline is a list of steps that shapes each record.

36
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:19,480
It parses, renames, adds and removes fields.

37
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,480
A destination is where records go.

38
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:25,080
A search tool, a topic, a bucket.

39
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:26,820
A route joins them.

40
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:32,200
It takes records from one source, through a pipeline, to its destinations.

41
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,990
Those parts need somewhere to run.

42
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:40,440
smeltr splits the work between two kinds of machine: workers, and a leader.

43
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:42,520
A worker moves the data.

44
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:47,380
It reads from sources, runs pipelines, and delivers to destinations.

45
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:50,430
You run as many workers as your data needs.

46
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:52,830
The leader holds everything else.

47
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:57,730
The configuration, the people who change it, and a picture of the whole fleet.

48
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:01,990
The configuration lives in a Git repository on the leader.

49
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:04,310
Every change you save is a commit.

50
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:08,930
When you deploy, every worker takes that one commit and runs it.

51
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:11,310
Workers fetch from the leader.

52
00:03:11,310 --> 00:03:14,000
The leader never reaches into a worker.

53
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:19,410
So a worker keeps moving data with the commit it has, even while the leader is down.

54
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:21,670
That's how smeltr is put together.

55
00:03:21,670 --> 00:03:24,430
Here's what you'll build with it in this course.

56
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:28,190
A Linux server sends its login logs over syslog.

57
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:33,510
A worker receives them, and a pipeline turns each line into fields you can search.

58
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:38,480
Then it delivers them to Elasticsearch, where you can search failed logins.

59
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:43,860
Everything here runs on Linux with Docker, so you can follow along on your own machines.

60
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:49,610
Before we check what stuck, let's spend a minute on why the pipeline layer matters.

61
00:03:49,610 --> 00:03:54,720
Connect every source straight to every tool, and you need a link for each pair.

62
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:57,570
Ten sources and five tools make fifty links,

63
00:03:57,570 --> 00:04:00,610
and each one is somebody's job to keep working.

64
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:06,000
Put the pipeline layer in between, and it drops to fifteen: one for each source,

65
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,380
and one for each tool.

66
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:11,100
Add an eleventh source, and that's one more link, not five.

67
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:13,350
This idea is older than smeltr.

68
00:04:13,350 --> 00:04:15,560
In Enterprise Integration Patterns,

69
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:21,100
Gregor Hohpe and Bobby Woolf call the shared shape in the middle a canonical data model.

70
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:22,800
It isn't free, though.

71
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:28,520
Every tool now depends on the pipeline layer, so if it stops, everything downstream waits.

72
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:31,950
smeltr's answer is to run as many workers as you need,

73
00:04:31,950 --> 00:04:35,960
and to let each keep moving data even while the leader is down.

74
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,180
So that's the shape of smeltr.

75
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:43,120
Sources bring records in, pipelines shape them, and destinations send them on.

76
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:46,920
Workers do the work, and the leader keeps the configuration.

77
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:48,250
Let's see what stuck.

78
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:51,720
Check on learning.

79
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:56,730
Which part of smeltr reads from sources and delivers to destinations?

80
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:02,690
A worker.

81
00:05:02,690 --> 00:05:06,850
The leader holds the configuration, and workers move the data.

82
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:10,010
The leader goes down.

83
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:13,000
What happens to the data your workers are moving?

84
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:19,550
It keeps flowing.

85
00:05:19,550 --> 00:05:22,550
Each worker runs the commit it already has.

86
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:28,060
Chapter two.

87
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:31,170
Install smeltr, and add a second worker.

88
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:33,860
The leader and the workers are separate,

89
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:38,170
and in production that is the point: you change configuration on the leader,

90
00:05:38,170 --> 00:05:39,980
and the data never waits for it.

91
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:42,670
To start, one machine runs both.

92
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:45,270
Then a second machine adds a worker.

93
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:51,510
That's the second practice: keep the control of your data apart from the data itself.

94
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:54,780
Before you install it, here's how smeltr ships.

95
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:59,290
There's a download for Linux and for Windows, and either needs only Git,

96
00:05:59,290 --> 00:06:00,800
on the leader's machine.

97
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:02,670
And there's a container image.

98
00:06:02,670 --> 00:06:07,870
This course uses the image: the program and everything it needs, packaged together,

99
00:06:07,870 --> 00:06:09,500
the same on every machine.

100
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:14,810
A container is that image, running, isolated from the rest of the machine.

101
00:06:14,810 --> 00:06:17,860
When it's replaced, its own files go with it.

102
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:21,250
A volume is storage that outlives the container.

103
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:25,890
That's where smeltr keeps its state, so an upgrade replaces the program,

104
00:06:25,890 --> 00:06:27,180
and keeps your data.

105
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:28,470
Now install it.

106
00:06:28,470 --> 00:06:32,620
On the first machine, make a folder for smeltr, and go into it.

107
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,260
Download the compose file of the release you want.

108
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:38,880
Here, that's 1.1.0.

109
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:42,410
It runs the leader and a worker in one container.

110
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:49,190
The container is read-only, and holds no privileges.

111
00:06:49,190 --> 00:06:51,440
smeltr writes only to its volume.

112
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:57,680
The leader serves HTTPS on port 443, with a certificate it signs itself.

113
00:06:58,040 --> 00:07:02,500
Everything it keeps goes in one volume, so back that volume up.

114
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:06,640
Two settings go in a file named .env, beside it.

115
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,640
The image, with the release's version.

116
00:07:09,640 --> 00:07:13,940
Pin the version, so a restart never upgrades you by surprise.

117
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:18,140
And the address your browser opens, with this machine's name.

118
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:19,230
Start it.

119
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:23,820
On its first start, smeltr creates an administrator,

120
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:26,590
and prints a one-time password in its logs.

121
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:27,400
Copy it.

122
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:31,460
smeltr prints it once, and Docker keeps it in the container's logs.

123
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:35,400
That's why your first sign-in makes you choose your own password.

124
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:36,510
Locked out?

125
00:07:36,510 --> 00:07:41,220
In compose.yaml, under environment, set SMELTR_RECOVER_ADMIN to true,

126
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:43,610
and run docker compose up -d again.

127
00:07:43,610 --> 00:07:48,790
It resets the admin account, and prints a new one-time password in the logs.

128
00:07:48,790 --> 00:07:53,230
Then remove the setting, or every start resets the account again.

129
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:55,620
Open the leader at its address.

130
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:57,360
The browser warns you.

131
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:02,740
The leader signed its own certificate, and the browser doesn't know who signed it.

132
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:06,160
For this first sign-in, go on to the leader.

133
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:10,210
In production, give it a certificate your browser trusts.

134
00:08:10,210 --> 00:08:13,190
The Trusted HTTPS course shows three ways.

135
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:16,990
Sign in as admin, with the one-time password.

136
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:20,450
smeltr asks you to choose your own password.

137
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:26,110
Then sign in again, with your new password.

138
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:29,690
Why did the browser warn?

139
00:08:29,690 --> 00:08:35,030
Your browser and your operating system ship with a list of certificate authorities they

140
00:08:35,030 --> 00:08:35,400
trust.

141
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:38,710
A website's certificate must be signed by one of them.

142
00:08:39,080 --> 00:08:44,010
Most sites have a chain: the site's certificate is signed by an intermediate,

143
00:08:44,010 --> 00:08:46,510
and the intermediate by a trusted root.

144
00:08:46,510 --> 00:08:50,550
The browser follows the chain until it reaches a root it knows.

145
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:55,560
Your leader made its own authority, and signed its own certificate with it.

146
00:08:55,560 --> 00:09:00,760
That authority isn't on anyone's list, so the chain ends nowhere the browser trusts.

147
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,200
The connection is still encrypted.

148
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:06,800
What the browser can't tell you is who's on the other end.

149
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:09,600
That's the part a trusted certificate proves.

150
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:12,690
Back in smeltr, Status opens on the fleet.

151
00:09:12,690 --> 00:09:17,880
The standalone worker group has one worker, and its check mark says it's online.

152
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:22,410
A worker group is a set of workers that run the same configuration.

153
00:09:22,410 --> 00:09:25,310
Every worker in this one runs the same routes.

154
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:28,360
Running shows the commit the worker runs.

155
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:31,620
When you deploy a change, it moves to the new one.

156
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:37,010
In production you run more than one worker, so the fleet can grow with your data.

157
00:09:37,010 --> 00:09:38,760
Add one on a second machine.

158
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:42,250
A new worker joins with an enrollment code.

159
00:09:42,250 --> 00:09:45,640
In Admin, open Workers, then Enrollment codes.

160
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,310
Add a code for the standalone group.

161
00:09:48,310 --> 00:09:51,840
It admits one worker, and expires in twenty-four hours.

162
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:56,450
Keep codes short-lived, and for as few workers as you need.

163
00:09:56,450 --> 00:09:58,630
A code is a key to your fleet.

164
00:09:59,000 --> 00:09:59,700
Mint it.

165
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:05,230
Check the address the worker reaches the leader at.

166
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:07,630
Then copy the settings it shows.

167
00:10:07,630 --> 00:10:09,500
They're shown only this once.

168
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:14,470
On the second machine, save them in a file named worker.env.

169
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:18,990
Start the worker from the same image as the leader, with that file,

170
00:10:18,990 --> 00:10:20,660
and a volume for its state.

171
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:22,890
Its logs show it enrolled.

172
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:25,940
The worker is enrolled.

173
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:30,170
No tuition, no textbooks, and it never skips class.

174
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:35,310
The code names the leader's certificate authority, so the worker takes that one,

175
00:10:35,310 --> 00:10:36,570
and accepts no other.

176
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:39,010
The code is spent once it's used.

177
00:10:39,010 --> 00:10:43,790
The worker now holds its own credential, so it never needs the code again,

178
00:10:43,790 --> 00:10:45,660
and nobody else can reuse it.

179
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:50,710
Back on Status, the standalone group has two workers, both online.

180
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:55,560
They're in one group, so both run its routes, at the same commit.

181
00:10:55,960 --> 00:10:59,110
Why keep the leader and the workers apart at all?

182
00:10:59,110 --> 00:11:02,000
Network engineers split routers the same way.

183
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,770
A control plane decides where traffic goes.

184
00:11:04,770 --> 00:11:06,180
A data plane moves it.

185
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:10,870
Software-defined networking made that split its founding idea.

186
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:15,490
Feamster, Rexford and Zegura trace its history in The Road to SDN.

187
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:17,370
Here's what that buys you.

188
00:11:17,370 --> 00:11:19,480
If the leader carried your data too,

189
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:24,300
every change you saved and every upgrade to the leader would hold up your records.

190
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:29,230
Kept apart, you can restart or upgrade the leader, and the workers keep moving data.

191
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:31,310
So that's smeltr installed.

192
00:11:31,310 --> 00:11:34,110
A leader with its own worker on one machine,

193
00:11:34,110 --> 00:11:38,480
a second worker enrolled with a one-time code, and both in one group,

194
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:40,010
running the same commit.

195
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,770
Let's see what you learned in our check on learning.

196
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:48,140
Where do you find the password for your first sign-in?

197
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:56,100
In the leader's logs, from its first start.

198
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:59,090
smeltr prints it once, and Docker keeps it there.

199
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:03,370
The leader signed its own certificate.

200
00:12:03,370 --> 00:12:05,490
Why does the new worker trust it?

201
00:12:10,840 --> 00:12:14,600
Its enrollment code names the leader's certificate authority,

202
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:16,890
and the worker accepts only that one.

203
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:22,720
Chapter three.

204
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:24,450
Bring in real logs.

205
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:28,070
This server logs every login to its auth log.

206
00:12:28,070 --> 00:12:30,450
Successful ones, and failed ones.

207
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:32,920
You want those lines in smeltr.

208
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:39,020
Syslog is how most servers and network devices send logs, so it's where most feeds start.

209
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:43,270
Before you write a single step, you'll capture real lines.

210
00:12:43,270 --> 00:12:47,380
A pipeline built from real lines handles what really arrives.

211
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:50,750
One built from memory handles what you remembered.

212
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:53,220
First, give the receiver a way in.

213
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:58,640
A receiver listens on every worker in its group, and every worker runs in a container.

214
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:03,800
Other machines can reach a port in a container only if the container publishes it.

215
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:08,650
The server will send to smeltr.example.com, the leader's machine.

216
00:13:08,650 --> 00:13:14,250
So publish port 6514, the port for syslog over TLS, in the leader's compose file,

217
00:13:14,250 --> 00:13:17,290
and the leader's own worker takes the lines.

218
00:13:17,290 --> 00:13:21,650
That's the one setting this course changes in the compose file.

219
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:26,800
The second worker listens on 6514 too, but its container publishes no port,

220
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:28,160
so no lines reach it.

221
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:33,070
That's fine here, since one server sends everything to the leader's machine.

222
00:13:33,070 --> 00:13:37,200
To share a busy feed, you'd publish the port on that worker too,

223
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,910
and send part of the feed to each machine.

224
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:42,170
Run docker compose up -d again.

225
00:13:42,170 --> 00:13:43,930
Docker sees the changed file,

226
00:13:43,930 --> 00:13:47,640
and recreates the leader's container with the port published.

227
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:51,670
The restart signs everyone out, so sign in again.

228
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:57,300
Login logs name your users and where they connect from.

229
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:00,340
Don't send them across the network in plain text.

230
00:14:00,340 --> 00:14:01,830
Encrypt them in transit.

231
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:05,190
For TLS, the receiver needs a certificate.

232
00:14:05,190 --> 00:14:07,040
Your leader can issue one.

233
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:10,360
Choose Issued by this leader.

234
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:14,460
Call it syslog, for the name the server reaches the worker by.

235
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:15,250
Add it.

236
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:20,840
The leader renews it on its own, before it expires, and workers take each new version.

237
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:25,440
Before you pick a transport, here's what each does on the wire.

238
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:29,230
TCP opens a connection first, with a three-way handshake.

239
00:14:29,230 --> 00:14:33,970
Then every byte is numbered, and the receiver acknowledges what it got.

240
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:37,190
Which is why TCP jokes take so long.

241
00:14:37,190 --> 00:14:40,340
Every punchline has to be acknowledged.

242
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:44,390
The receiver also says how much more it has room for.

243
00:14:44,390 --> 00:14:45,780
That's TCP's window.

244
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:51,260
When the receiver stops reading, the window closes, and the sender has to wait.

245
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:53,220
UDP has none of that.

246
00:14:53,220 --> 00:14:56,710
No connection, no acknowledgement, no window.

247
00:14:56,710 --> 00:15:02,280
Each message is one datagram, sent once, whether anyone is ready or not.

248
00:15:02,640 --> 00:15:05,930
UDP is a postcard thrown over the fence.

249
00:15:05,930 --> 00:15:09,790
Whether anyone catches it is not UDP's problem.

250
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:12,710
Now open Build, and add a source.

251
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:17,540
Syslog is a push source.

252
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:21,610
The server sends, and every worker in the group listens.

253
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:23,890
Pick Syslog receiver.

254
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:25,580
Call it linux-auth.

255
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:29,720
Name a source for what it carries, not for where it runs.

256
00:15:30,080 --> 00:15:33,100
Port 6514, over TCP.

257
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:36,550
Use TCP wherever the sender can send it.

258
00:15:36,550 --> 00:15:40,150
Over UDP nothing tells the sender to slow down,

259
00:15:40,150 --> 00:15:43,530
so a busy receiver drops what it can't take.

260
00:15:43,530 --> 00:15:46,680
And TLS needs a stream, which UDP is not.

261
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:50,670
Give it the certificate syslog, so it serves TLS.

262
00:15:51,080 --> 00:15:55,400
Syslog carries no password, so smeltr asks who may send.

263
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:57,950
Allow only this server's address.

264
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:02,340
Anyone a receiver admits can put records into your pipelines.

265
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:04,470
Admit only the senders you know.

266
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:06,510
Add it, and save.

267
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:13,990
Main changes only when you deploy, so smeltr asks for a branch.

268
00:16:13,990 --> 00:16:17,070
Call it linux-auth, for the change it carries.

269
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:18,990
Here's what TLS adds.

270
00:16:18,990 --> 00:16:23,220
When rsyslog connects, the worker sends its certificate.

271
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:28,140
rsyslog checks two things: that an authority it trusts signed it,

272
00:16:28,140 --> 00:16:32,450
and that it was issued for the name rsyslog connected to.

273
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:34,780
Once the certificate checks out,

274
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:39,190
everything after that is encrypted with keys only the two of them hold.

275
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:43,300
That's why the sending server needs the leader's authority.

276
00:16:43,300 --> 00:16:48,300
Without it, rsyslog can't tell the real worker from anyone else on the network.

277
00:16:48,640 --> 00:16:52,040
On the server, fetch the leader's certificate authority,

278
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:54,520
so rsyslog can check who it's talking to.

279
00:16:54,880 --> 00:16:59,490
Check its fingerprint against the one the leader shows, before you trust it.

280
00:17:08,840 --> 00:17:12,100
Then tell rsyslog to forward the auth log.

281
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:13,420
One file does it.

282
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:17,810
auth and authpriv are the logs of logins and sudo.

283
00:17:18,160 --> 00:17:20,830
It sends over TLS, to port 6514,

284
00:17:20,830 --> 00:17:27,910
and accepts only a certificate the leader's authority signed, for smeltr.example.com.

285
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:32,300
On a stream, the receiver needs to know where one message ends.

286
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:35,970
Ending each at a newline breaks a message that holds one.

287
00:17:35,970 --> 00:17:40,150
Octet counting puts each message's length in front of it instead,

288
00:17:40,150 --> 00:17:42,340
so a message arrives in one piece.

289
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:47,330
And a queue that spills to disk holds messages while smeltr is out of reach,

290
00:17:47,330 --> 00:17:48,720
instead of losing them.

291
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:52,060
Plain syslog never confirms that a message was handled,

292
00:17:52,060 --> 00:17:54,540
so the sender's queue is your safety net.

293
00:17:54,920 --> 00:17:56,860
Restart rsyslog.

294
00:17:57,760 --> 00:18:00,820
A capture reads what a running receiver takes,

295
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:03,750
and a receiver runs only once it's deployed.

296
00:18:03,750 --> 00:18:05,010
So deploy it first.

297
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:07,230
Add a route from linux-auth.

298
00:18:07,230 --> 00:18:12,200
A route takes records from a source, through a pipeline, to destinations.

299
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:17,100
Give it a new pipeline, called linux-logins.

300
00:18:17,100 --> 00:18:19,470
It stays empty until chapter four.

301
00:18:26,200 --> 00:18:27,990
It has no destination yet.

302
00:18:27,990 --> 00:18:30,820
A record with nowhere to go is Unmatched,

303
00:18:30,820 --> 00:18:35,650
and Unmatched blocks by default: the receiver would stop taking lines.

304
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,110
So set Unmatched to drop, for now.

305
00:18:38,110 --> 00:18:42,630
Nothing needs these lines yet, and the server keeps them in its own file.

306
00:18:42,630 --> 00:18:45,110
Chapter five gives them somewhere to go.

307
00:18:45,480 --> 00:18:48,400
Save, open it for review, and deploy it.

308
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:51,090
Chapter five reads a review properly.

309
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:08,360
Back in smeltr, capture a sample from linux-auth.

310
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:15,620
Pick the standalone group and the source.

311
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:21,160
Take at most fifty records, for two minutes.

312
00:19:21,520 --> 00:19:22,200
Start it.

313
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:26,620
Now give the capture some logins.

314
00:19:26,620 --> 00:19:32,760
On the server, log in to it over SSH, as a user that has a password, at its own address.

315
00:19:32,760 --> 00:19:34,020
Here that's alice.

316
00:19:34,020 --> 00:19:37,780
Get the password wrong twice, then right, and log out.

317
00:19:37,780 --> 00:19:42,250
That puts two failed logins and one that worked in the auth log.

318
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:44,770
The lines arrive as they happen.

319
00:19:46,880 --> 00:19:47,860
That's enough.

320
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:49,060
Stop the capture.

321
00:19:49,920 --> 00:19:52,100
Save it as linux-auth-logins.

322
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:56,010
A sample is configuration too, so it needs a branch.

323
00:19:56,010 --> 00:19:58,040
Call this one linux-logins.

324
00:19:58,040 --> 00:20:00,600
The next two chapters build on it.

325
00:20:00,960 --> 00:20:02,430
Read what arrived.

326
00:20:02,430 --> 00:20:04,640
Each message is one record.

327
00:20:04,640 --> 00:20:08,810
Its _raw is the line exactly as the server sent it.

328
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:12,700
It starts with a number in angle brackets: the priority,

329
00:20:12,700 --> 00:20:16,120
which packs the facility and severity into one number.

330
00:20:16,480 --> 00:20:21,810
Then the time, the host, and the program that wrote it: sshd.

331
00:20:22,120 --> 00:20:23,610
And then the message.

332
00:20:23,610 --> 00:20:25,870
This is the part you care about.

333
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:31,420
Alice, as security examples demand, is having trouble with the password.

334
00:20:31,760 --> 00:20:35,110
smeltr also notes the address the line came from.

335
00:20:35,560 --> 00:20:38,270
None of it is a field you can search yet.

336
00:20:38,270 --> 00:20:39,330
It's one string.

337
00:20:39,330 --> 00:20:41,580
Chapter four turns it into fields.

338
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:43,260
Look down the sample.

339
00:20:43,260 --> 00:20:47,980
Accepted lines and failed lines say different things, in different orders.

340
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:50,080
Your pipeline has to handle both.

341
00:20:50,400 --> 00:20:52,760
And most lines aren't logins at all.

342
00:20:52,760 --> 00:20:55,790
Sessions open and close, and connections drop.

343
00:20:55,790 --> 00:20:59,000
A sample shows you the lines you didn't think of.

344
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:02,540
Before we build on these lines, a little history.

345
00:21:02,540 --> 00:21:06,100
Syslog is older than most of the machines that send it.

346
00:21:06,100 --> 00:21:10,510
Eric Allman wrote it in the 1980s, as part of sendmail, at Berkeley.

347
00:21:10,840 --> 00:21:13,470
For years, nobody wrote it down.

348
00:21:13,470 --> 00:21:18,400
In 2001, RFC 3164 described what senders were already doing.

349
00:21:18,400 --> 00:21:25,210
It describes, rather than requires, so every vendor's syslog is a little different.

350
00:21:25,560 --> 00:21:33,460
In 2009, RFC 5424 set a real standard, with a full timestamp and a year.

351
00:21:33,460 --> 00:21:41,570
RFC 5425 added TLS, and RFC 6587 described how to frame messages over TCP.

352
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:46,580
rsyslog still forwards in the old format unless you tell it otherwise.

353
00:21:46,580 --> 00:21:49,870
The server's own file has a year and a time zone.

354
00:21:49,870 --> 00:21:51,880
What you captured has neither.

355
00:21:52,200 --> 00:21:56,950
Those two formats still travel side by side, and senders mix them freely.

356
00:21:56,950 --> 00:22:00,010
So receivers learned to accept almost anything.

357
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:03,670
That habit has a name: the robustness principle.

358
00:22:03,670 --> 00:22:08,150
Be conservative in what you send, and liberal in what you accept.

359
00:22:08,150 --> 00:22:11,880
Jon Postel wrote it into the early internet standards.

360
00:22:11,880 --> 00:22:14,770
A later RFC, Maintaining Robust Protocols,

361
00:22:14,770 --> 00:22:19,460
argues that accepting everything lets faulty senders go uncorrected.

362
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:22,470
By default, smeltr takes a middle path.

363
00:22:22,470 --> 00:22:25,570
A line your pipeline can't read still goes on,

364
00:22:25,570 --> 00:22:30,630
with the original line and what went wrong beside it, and smeltr counts it.

365
00:22:30,630 --> 00:22:33,470
So you find out, not the people searching.

366
00:22:33,470 --> 00:22:35,490
Chapter seven shows it happen.

367
00:22:35,840 --> 00:22:38,070
So that's a real feed coming in.

368
00:22:38,070 --> 00:22:43,140
rsyslog sends the server's logins over TLS, a Syslog receiver takes them,

369
00:22:43,140 --> 00:22:45,990
and you've captured a sample to build on.

370
00:22:48,360 --> 00:22:50,220
Time for a quick check on learning.

371
00:22:50,600 --> 00:22:52,700
Syslog carries no password.

372
00:22:52,700 --> 00:22:56,820
How does a Syslog receiver decide who may send to it?

373
00:23:02,200 --> 00:23:04,190
By the addresses you allow.

374
00:23:06,040 --> 00:23:09,430
Why capture a sample before you build the pipeline?

375
00:23:14,760 --> 00:23:19,080
So the pipeline is built and checked against the lines that really arrive.

376
00:23:23,600 --> 00:23:24,860
Chapter four.

377
00:23:24,860 --> 00:23:27,090
Turn lines into fields.

378
00:23:27,480 --> 00:23:30,860
A security team searching logins wants fields.

379
00:23:30,860 --> 00:23:34,450
Who tried to log in, from where, and did it work.

380
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:37,170
But keep the line as it arrived.

381
00:23:37,170 --> 00:23:42,650
When someone asks what really happened, the original line is the evidence.

382
00:23:42,650 --> 00:23:45,170
Shape a copy, never the only copy.

383
00:23:45,480 --> 00:23:49,730
And parse once, here, so no tool downstream has to.

384
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:51,070
Let's build it.

385
00:23:51,070 --> 00:23:55,680
Open the pipeline linux-logins, the empty one your route already runs.

386
00:23:57,080 --> 00:24:02,720
Open it on the sample you captured, so every step shows its output on real lines.

387
00:24:03,080 --> 00:24:05,950
Before the first step, here's how it works.

388
00:24:05,950 --> 00:24:09,350
Parsing a line means matching it against a pattern.

389
00:24:09,350 --> 00:24:14,880
A regular expression describes text: this many digits, then a space, then any word.

390
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:18,500
Grok gives common regular expressions names.

391
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:24,420
IP matches an address, INT a whole number, and you name what each one captures.

392
00:24:24,760 --> 00:24:31,100
Grok comes from science fiction, where it means to understand something completely.

393
00:24:31,100 --> 00:24:34,230
Your pattern only has to understand sshd.

394
00:24:34,230 --> 00:24:35,150
Much easier.

395
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:38,070
A pattern is tried from left to right.

396
00:24:38,070 --> 00:24:40,480
Make it as exact as the line allows.

397
00:24:40,480 --> 00:24:45,910
A loose pattern matches lines it shouldn't, and fills fields with the wrong text.

398
00:24:46,280 --> 00:24:51,930
Jamie Zawinski once warned that a person who solves a problem with a regular expression

399
00:24:51,930 --> 00:24:53,290
now has two problems.

400
00:24:53,290 --> 00:24:55,880
Keep your patterns small, and test them.

401
00:24:56,200 --> 00:24:57,790
Back to the pipeline.

402
00:24:57,790 --> 00:25:02,800
Add a Parser step that reads _raw, with the Regex and Grok format.

403
00:25:07,560 --> 00:25:09,750
Grok names the pieces of a line.

404
00:25:09,750 --> 00:25:14,550
This pattern reads the syslog header: the time, the host, the program,

405
00:25:14,550 --> 00:25:16,270
and the message after it.

406
00:25:19,520 --> 00:25:22,750
Some programs, like sudo, write no process ID,

407
00:25:22,750 --> 00:25:28,640
so the process ID part of the pattern is optional: parentheses, and a question mark.

408
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:33,730
The output shows each piece as its own field, on every line of the sample.

409
00:25:37,560 --> 00:25:38,920
Every line matched.

410
00:25:38,920 --> 00:25:43,410
The header is the same on every line, because rsyslog wrote it.

411
00:25:43,760 --> 00:25:46,750
Most of these lines aren't login results.

412
00:25:46,750 --> 00:25:50,100
Sessions open and close, and connections drop.

413
00:25:50,100 --> 00:25:54,330
Drop them first, so the steps after see only what matters.

414
00:25:54,680 --> 00:25:55,910
Add a Drop step.

415
00:25:55,910 --> 00:26:01,060
Its condition names each kind: sshd lines that start with pam_unix,

416
00:26:01,060 --> 00:26:04,220
Received disconnect or Disconnected from.

417
00:26:04,220 --> 00:26:08,520
It names one more kind too: lines that end with preauth,

418
00:26:08,520 --> 00:26:13,680
which sshd writes when a connection closes before a login finishes.

419
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:17,600
They're noise as well, and chapter seven shows one.

420
00:26:17,920 --> 00:26:20,720
Name what you drop, never what you keep.

421
00:26:20,720 --> 00:26:25,820
Then a line you've never seen still reaches the parser, and it tells you.

422
00:26:25,820 --> 00:26:27,850
Chapter seven counts on that.

423
00:26:28,200 --> 00:26:31,080
The noise is gone, and the logins remain.

424
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:33,140
Now the message.

425
00:26:33,140 --> 00:26:38,840
Add a second Parser step that reads message, only on sshd's lines.

426
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:49,650
A filter runs a step only on the records where it's true.

427
00:26:49,650 --> 00:26:53,900
Other programs write other messages, so they keep just their header.

428
00:26:54,280 --> 00:26:57,550
One pattern reads the lines that say Accepted.

429
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:09,940
A second reads the lines that say Failed password.

430
00:27:13,480 --> 00:27:18,070
smeltr tries the patterns in order, and keeps the first that matches.

431
00:27:18,440 --> 00:27:23,020
Now each login line has the user, and the source address and port.

432
00:27:24,440 --> 00:27:26,310
The port is already an integer.

433
00:27:26,310 --> 00:27:31,570
Its pattern reads a whole number, and smeltr types a field by the pattern that read it.

434
00:27:31,570 --> 00:27:34,830
Then a search can compare it, not just match its text.

435
00:27:35,200 --> 00:27:37,730
Then add an Eval step for the outcome,

436
00:27:37,730 --> 00:27:42,590
on the lines with a user: success where the message starts with Accepted,

437
00:27:42,590 --> 00:27:44,250
failure where it doesn't.

438
00:27:49,840 --> 00:27:50,760
Now the time.

439
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:56,540
Every source writes time its own way, and a datetime is one shape for all of them.

440
00:27:56,880 --> 00:28:00,230
The header step made time a datetime the same way.

441
00:28:00,230 --> 00:28:03,110
Its pattern reads this common syslog shape,

442
00:28:03,110 --> 00:28:07,270
so smeltr also filled in how to read it: the format on screen.

443
00:28:07,640 --> 00:28:10,730
This format has no year, and no time zone.

444
00:28:10,730 --> 00:28:16,610
smeltr takes the current year, or last year for a December line read in January.

445
00:28:16,610 --> 00:28:18,750
And it reads the time as UTC.

446
00:28:19,080 --> 00:28:22,730
So set your servers' clocks to UTC wherever you can.

447
00:28:22,730 --> 00:28:26,450
A log that mixes zones is a timeline nobody can read.

448
00:28:26,760 --> 00:28:29,230
Remember the number in angle brackets.

449
00:28:29,230 --> 00:28:32,750
The header pattern read it as pri, short for priority.

450
00:28:32,750 --> 00:28:38,020
Like the port, it's already an integer, so the next step can do arithmetic on it.

451
00:28:38,360 --> 00:28:40,490
It packs two numbers into one.

452
00:28:40,490 --> 00:28:44,100
The facility is pri divided by eight, rounded down.

453
00:28:44,100 --> 00:28:46,440
The severity is what's left over.

454
00:28:46,760 --> 00:28:49,380
Add an Eval step, and work both out.

455
00:29:02,640 --> 00:29:04,730
Severity six is informational.

456
00:29:04,730 --> 00:29:08,980
A search for warnings and worse can now filter on one number.

457
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:11,930
Name fields for the people who search them.

458
00:29:11,930 --> 00:29:16,700
Use the names your security tool expects, so a search works across every source.

459
00:29:17,040 --> 00:29:21,180
Naming things is one of the two hard problems in computing.

460
00:29:21,180 --> 00:29:25,310
The other one is cache invalidation, and off-by-one errors.

461
00:29:25,680 --> 00:29:31,450
Security teams solve naming with a shared schema: one agreed name for each kind of field,

462
00:29:31,450 --> 00:29:33,140
whatever product wrote it.

463
00:29:33,140 --> 00:29:36,120
Two common ones are the Elastic Common Schema,

464
00:29:36,120 --> 00:29:38,970
and the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework.

465
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:42,860
The names you'll use here, user.name and source.ip,

466
00:29:42,860 --> 00:29:45,940
are the ones the Elastic Common Schema uses.

467
00:29:45,940 --> 00:29:49,030
smeltr can hold a pipeline to either schema.

468
00:29:49,030 --> 00:29:53,870
The video Hold to a schema, in the Shape your data course, shows how.

469
00:29:54,200 --> 00:29:55,890
Add a Rename step.

470
00:29:55,890 --> 00:30:04,230
user becomes user.name, source_ip becomes source.ip, and source_port becomes source.port.

471
00:30:16,200 --> 00:30:20,950
And outcome becomes event.outcome, and time becomes @timestamp,

472
00:30:20,950 --> 00:30:24,410
where Elasticsearch's tools look for the time.

473
00:30:27,480 --> 00:30:33,460
And _raw becomes event.original, the standard name for the line as it arrived.

474
00:30:33,760 --> 00:30:38,860
The dot puts each one inside an object: one user, one source.

475
00:30:39,160 --> 00:30:41,720
Then remove what nobody searches.

476
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:46,920
The pid, pri and the copy of the message add bytes to every record,

477
00:30:46,920 --> 00:30:48,700
and answer no question.

478
00:30:49,040 --> 00:30:52,830
The original line stays, now as event.original.

479
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:57,070
Pause the records after any step to see them as they leave it.

480
00:30:57,070 --> 00:31:00,040
That's how you find which step changed a field.

481
00:31:09,960 --> 00:31:11,160
Save the pipeline.

482
00:31:12,960 --> 00:31:17,690
Lock the result as a test, and give it a name that says what it checks.

483
00:31:17,690 --> 00:31:21,820
From now on, every change to this pipeline must still give it.

484
00:31:24,960 --> 00:31:28,110
Why keep the original line beside the fields you made?

485
00:31:28,110 --> 00:31:32,540
It's a question of provenance: being able to say where each value came from.

486
00:31:32,880 --> 00:31:36,160
Database researchers split it into two questions.

487
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:40,320
Why is this record here, and where was this value copied from?

488
00:31:40,320 --> 00:31:43,460
Buneman, Khanna and Tan set out the difference.

489
00:31:43,840 --> 00:31:46,270
There's a trade-off in where you parse.

490
00:31:46,270 --> 00:31:50,010
smeltr parses at the door, so every search gets your fields.

491
00:31:50,010 --> 00:31:54,690
Systems that parse at search time can apply a new pattern to old lines too.

492
00:31:54,690 --> 00:32:00,300
Keeping the original beside your fields means a change of format never costs you the line.

493
00:32:00,640 --> 00:32:03,240
So that's a raw line turned into fields.

494
00:32:03,240 --> 00:32:08,880
Parsers read the header and the message, the noise is dropped, the time is a real time,

495
00:32:08,880 --> 00:32:12,060
and the fields carry the names your tools expect.

496
00:32:12,060 --> 00:32:13,290
A test locks it in.

497
00:32:13,290 --> 00:32:16,280
Now, let's see how much of that you've parsed.

498
00:32:18,640 --> 00:32:19,880
Check on learning.

499
00:32:20,200 --> 00:32:23,600
Your pipeline parses each line into fields.

500
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:25,490
Why keep event.original?

501
00:32:30,880 --> 00:32:35,050
It's the original line, the evidence of what really arrived.

502
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:40,940
A Parser step has two patterns, and a line matches both.

503
00:32:40,940 --> 00:32:42,240
Which one is used?

504
00:32:47,600 --> 00:32:49,330
The first one in the list.

505
00:32:51,200 --> 00:32:54,790
Why does it matter that the time is a datetime, not text?

506
00:33:00,160 --> 00:33:03,600
A datetime has one standard form, ISO 8601,

507
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:08,080
with the year and time zone the line left out filled in.

508
00:33:12,600 --> 00:33:13,850
Chapter five.

509
00:33:13,850 --> 00:33:15,770
Deliver, and deploy.

510
00:33:16,120 --> 00:33:18,460
On the route, add a destination.

511
00:33:18,460 --> 00:33:20,790
A new Elasticsearch destination.

512
00:33:30,920 --> 00:33:32,050
Call it search.

513
00:33:32,050 --> 00:33:36,440
Give it the cluster's address, and the index linux-logins.

514
00:33:36,880 --> 00:33:40,530
Elasticsearch signed its own certificate, as your leader did.

515
00:33:40,530 --> 00:33:45,730
Paste its authority under trusted authorities, so smeltr can check who it's talking to.

516
00:33:46,040 --> 00:33:50,330
It signs in with an API key, from the secret es-api-key.

517
00:33:50,330 --> 00:33:54,620
The configuration is in Git, and secrets never go there.

518
00:33:54,960 --> 00:33:58,580
The key may only add documents to this one index.

519
00:33:58,580 --> 00:34:01,030
Give each key the least it needs.

520
00:34:01,360 --> 00:34:05,820
An index is where Elasticsearch stores documents, and what you search.

521
00:34:05,820 --> 00:34:09,520
One index for login records keeps their fields consistent.

522
00:34:09,840 --> 00:34:11,830
smeltr delivers at least once.

523
00:34:11,830 --> 00:34:16,740
When Elasticsearch doesn't confirm a batch in time, smeltr sends it again.

524
00:34:16,740 --> 00:34:20,320
If the first copy did arrive, a record is there twice.

525
00:34:20,680 --> 00:34:23,780
Syslog can't resend what a worker already took.

526
00:34:23,780 --> 00:34:27,940
What a worker holds and hasn't delivered when it stops is lost,

527
00:34:27,940 --> 00:34:30,850
because plain syslog has no acknowledgement.

528
00:34:31,160 --> 00:34:33,640
Bulk action says what to do about that.

529
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:37,650
When a document arrives with an ID that's already in the index,

530
00:34:37,650 --> 00:34:41,980
create keeps the first copy, and index replaces it with the new one.

531
00:34:42,360 --> 00:34:45,960
Neither helps without a stable ID in each record.

532
00:34:45,960 --> 00:34:51,180
A login line carries none, so Elasticsearch gives each copy its own ID.

533
00:34:51,180 --> 00:34:53,020
Keep create, the default.

534
00:34:53,020 --> 00:34:56,330
A rare duplicate is better than a lost login.

535
00:34:56,640 --> 00:34:58,980
Now every record has somewhere to go.

536
00:34:58,980 --> 00:35:04,670
But a later change could leave one with nowhere, and Unmatched decides what happens to it.

537
00:35:04,670 --> 00:35:07,710
Block, the default, holds it and stops the feed,

538
00:35:07,710 --> 00:35:11,700
and it still couldn't be delivered until you add a rule for it.

539
00:35:11,700 --> 00:35:14,100
Chapter seven shows when that's right.

540
00:35:14,480 --> 00:35:17,180
So set Unmatched to deliver, to search.

541
00:35:17,180 --> 00:35:22,020
Such a record still arrives, tagged with what went wrong, and counted,

542
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:23,890
and the feed keeps flowing.

543
00:35:24,200 --> 00:35:28,700
The route reads linux-auth, through linux-logins, to search.

544
00:35:28,700 --> 00:35:29,300
Save it.

545
00:35:30,200 --> 00:35:33,800
Before anything runs, check where the records go.

546
00:35:33,800 --> 00:35:35,050
Route the sample.

547
00:35:35,360 --> 00:35:39,100
Every login line would reach search, with its fields.

548
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:41,370
The noise stops at the pipeline.

549
00:35:41,370 --> 00:35:42,570
Nothing was sent.

550
00:35:43,560 --> 00:35:45,500
Nothing you saved runs yet.

551
00:35:45,500 --> 00:35:47,230
It's all on your branch.

552
00:35:47,230 --> 00:35:52,900
Workers run main, and main changes only when a change is reviewed and deployed.

553
00:35:53,280 --> 00:35:56,680
Review is the second pair of eyes every change gets.

554
00:35:56,680 --> 00:35:59,030
On a team, someone else approves it.

555
00:35:59,030 --> 00:36:01,650
Here, you read it as someone else would.

556
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:05,890
A second reader finds the typo you've read past six times.

557
00:36:06,240 --> 00:36:09,720
smeltr fills in the title and description from what changed.

558
00:36:10,600 --> 00:36:11,720
Open it for review.

559
00:36:12,320 --> 00:36:15,070
Read through what you're about to deploy.

560
00:36:15,070 --> 00:36:19,420
It adds the pipeline's steps, a sample, a test and a destination,

561
00:36:19,420 --> 00:36:21,700
and the route now sends to search.

562
00:36:21,700 --> 00:36:23,100
Nothing else changes.

563
00:36:23,480 --> 00:36:26,280
Read the pipeline's steps, as they'll run.

564
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:35,730
Approve and deploy it.

565
00:36:38,720 --> 00:36:39,690
No pressure.

566
00:36:39,690 --> 00:36:44,030
Well, a little backpressure, but that's chapter seven.

567
00:36:44,360 --> 00:36:45,960
smeltr opens Status.

568
00:36:45,960 --> 00:36:48,520
Both workers run the new commit.

569
00:36:50,120 --> 00:36:52,060
Map shows the route running.

570
00:36:59,360 --> 00:37:02,080
On the server, log in as alice again.

571
00:37:02,080 --> 00:37:03,840
Wrong twice, then right.

572
00:37:14,800 --> 00:37:18,020
Then ask Elasticsearch for the failed logins.

573
00:37:23,280 --> 00:37:24,240
There they are.

574
00:37:24,240 --> 00:37:28,850
The user, the address they came from, and the original line beside them.

575
00:37:29,800 --> 00:37:31,840
Here's what a deploy really is.

576
00:37:31,840 --> 00:37:36,770
Every save you made is a Git commit: a snapshot of the whole configuration,

577
00:37:36,770 --> 00:37:40,720
named by a hash of its contents and of the commit before it.

578
00:37:41,040 --> 00:37:44,290
Change one character, and the hash changes.

579
00:37:44,290 --> 00:37:48,530
So a hash names exactly one configuration, and no other.

580
00:37:48,880 --> 00:37:51,690
Each commit also names the one before it.

581
00:37:51,690 --> 00:37:56,140
That chain is the history of every change, who made it, and when.

582
00:37:56,440 --> 00:37:59,660
A deploy moves one pointer to one commit of main.

583
00:37:59,660 --> 00:38:04,720
Every worker fetches that commit and runs it, so they all run the same thing,

584
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:05,640
byte for byte.

585
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:09,560
So undoing a deploy is moving that pointer back.

586
00:38:09,560 --> 00:38:10,990
Nothing is deleted.

587
00:38:10,990 --> 00:38:13,090
Know how before you need it.

588
00:38:13,440 --> 00:38:17,970
Three in the morning is a poor time to read the rollback docs for the first time.

589
00:38:18,280 --> 00:38:20,940
Roll back to the commit before this one.

590
00:38:27,280 --> 00:38:32,520
The workers move back to chapter three's commit, where the route had no destination.

591
00:38:32,520 --> 00:38:34,210
Nothing new reaches search.

592
00:38:38,880 --> 00:38:43,180
The rollback holds the fleet there, even when new changes are approved,

593
00:38:43,180 --> 00:38:44,450
until you release it.

594
00:38:47,280 --> 00:38:52,000
Release it, and the fleet moves back to main's newest commit, with your route.

595
00:38:57,240 --> 00:39:00,140
You've reviewed every change before it ran.

596
00:39:00,140 --> 00:39:01,900
Does that slow teams down?

597
00:39:01,900 --> 00:39:06,020
Research on software delivery says it depends on who reviews.

598
00:39:06,320 --> 00:39:07,900
In Accelerate, Forsgren,

599
00:39:07,900 --> 00:39:13,630
Humble and Kim found that approval by an outside board went with slower delivery and no

600
00:39:13,630 --> 00:39:14,620
fewer failures.

601
00:39:14,620 --> 00:39:18,440
Review by a peer, inside the team, went with no such cost.

602
00:39:18,760 --> 00:39:23,480
So for a security feed, ask a peer who knows the feed, not an outside board.

603
00:39:23,480 --> 00:39:28,940
They check where the records go, what the pipeline drops, and that the tests still pass.

604
00:39:29,280 --> 00:39:31,120
So that's a change delivered.

605
00:39:31,120 --> 00:39:36,580
The route sends the shaped logins to Elasticsearch, a review let the change into main,

606
00:39:36,580 --> 00:39:39,000
and every worker runs that one commit.

607
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:41,600
If a deploy goes wrong, you roll it back.

608
00:39:43,960 --> 00:39:46,430
Over to you now, for the check on learning.

609
00:39:46,760 --> 00:39:52,170
Where does the Elasticsearch API key live, and why not in the configuration?

610
00:39:57,560 --> 00:39:59,440
In a secret on the leader.

611
00:39:59,440 --> 00:40:03,490
The configuration is in Git, and secrets never go there.

612
00:40:05,360 --> 00:40:06,960
A deploy goes wrong.

613
00:40:06,960 --> 00:40:08,480
How do you undo it?

614
00:40:13,840 --> 00:40:16,520
Roll back to the commit before it, in History.

615
00:40:16,520 --> 00:40:20,840
The workers move back to that commit, and stay there until you release it.

616
00:40:25,360 --> 00:40:26,610
Chapter six.

617
00:40:26,610 --> 00:40:27,970
Watch it run.

618
00:40:28,360 --> 00:40:32,330
You can't spot what's wrong until you know what right looks like.

619
00:40:32,330 --> 00:40:36,780
Spend a minute with a healthy feed now, so a broken one stands out later.

620
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:39,550
A real server's logins come in all day.

621
00:40:39,550 --> 00:40:42,420
Here, a loop on the server stands in for that.

622
00:40:42,420 --> 00:40:46,600
It logs in every few seconds, so there's always something to watch.

623
00:40:46,600 --> 00:40:50,460
On a quiet server, log in a few times yourself while you look.

624
00:40:50,800 --> 00:40:53,670
Status lists every worker, by worker group.

625
00:40:53,670 --> 00:40:56,940
Both workers are online, and run the same commit.

626
00:40:59,440 --> 00:41:02,440
Rows in and out shows the whole fleet's flow.

627
00:41:02,440 --> 00:41:05,250
Above the line are rows read from sources.

628
00:41:05,250 --> 00:41:07,850
Below it are rows sent to destinations.

629
00:41:08,160 --> 00:41:11,290
In this feed, fewer rows go out than come in.

630
00:41:11,290 --> 00:41:15,740
The pipeline drops the noise, and every login goes on to search.

631
00:41:16,560 --> 00:41:20,030
Each worker shows its own share, and what it's busy with.

632
00:41:21,240 --> 00:41:25,310
Syslog arrives on one connection, so one worker takes all of it.

633
00:41:25,310 --> 00:41:26,900
The other waits for work.

634
00:41:28,040 --> 00:41:33,720
Map traces the flow, from each source, through its pipelines, to each destination.

635
00:41:37,400 --> 00:41:39,970
Each one shows its rows per second.

636
00:41:41,400 --> 00:41:43,010
Select one to see more.

637
00:41:43,010 --> 00:41:46,790
linux-logins shows how many rows came in and went out.

638
00:41:46,790 --> 00:41:52,530
Its Steps tab shows how many each step dropped, and how many carried an exception.

639
00:41:52,840 --> 00:41:54,560
Right now that's zero.

640
00:41:54,560 --> 00:41:55,500
Remember it.

641
00:41:55,500 --> 00:41:57,850
In chapter seven, it won't be.

642
00:41:58,240 --> 00:42:01,200
Issues is where smeltr lists what needs you.

643
00:42:01,200 --> 00:42:03,960
An empty list is what healthy looks like.

644
00:42:04,760 --> 00:42:07,030
A healthy feed is boring.

645
00:42:07,030 --> 00:42:08,760
Boring is the goal.

646
00:42:09,080 --> 00:42:13,700
When something breaks, a red banner on every page lists the active issues,

647
00:42:13,700 --> 00:42:15,820
so you don't have to come looking.

648
00:42:16,200 --> 00:42:18,890
Logs give the detail behind an issue.

649
00:42:18,890 --> 00:42:21,360
Every worker's logs, in one place.

650
00:42:21,720 --> 00:42:23,560
Choose the levels you want.

651
00:42:23,560 --> 00:42:25,330
Errors and warnings first.

652
00:42:27,720 --> 00:42:32,570
Add info to see what workers do in normal running, such as taking a new deploy.

653
00:42:32,880 --> 00:42:35,960
Open the pipeline to read it as it runs.

654
00:42:35,960 --> 00:42:38,510
Each step, in order, as deployed.

655
00:42:39,760 --> 00:42:43,890
Pick a record, and follow it from _raw to what search received.

656
00:42:43,890 --> 00:42:47,500
That's how you answer "where did this field come from".

657
00:42:56,320 --> 00:43:00,850
Before we check what stuck, one idea that helps you read a feed's numbers.

658
00:43:00,850 --> 00:43:03,540
Queueing theory ties three numbers together.

659
00:43:03,540 --> 00:43:07,770
The average number of records waiting equals the rate they arrive at,

660
00:43:07,770 --> 00:43:10,030
times how long each waits on average.

661
00:43:10,400 --> 00:43:16,620
Say logins arrive at twenty a second, and each one spends half a second inside smeltr.

662
00:43:16,620 --> 00:43:20,020
On average, ten are on their way at any moment.

663
00:43:20,320 --> 00:43:24,950
Now the destination answers slower, and each record waits five seconds.

664
00:43:24,950 --> 00:43:30,630
smeltr sends records in batches, several at once, so twenty a second still get through.

665
00:43:30,630 --> 00:43:32,980
At that rate, a hundred are waiting.

666
00:43:32,980 --> 00:43:36,240
Nothing about the arrivals changed, only the wait.

667
00:43:36,560 --> 00:43:40,280
It's called Little's law, after John Little, who proved it.

668
00:43:40,280 --> 00:43:43,760
It holds for any queue that keeps up with its arrivals,

669
00:43:43,760 --> 00:43:47,160
from a checkout line to the records waiting in smeltr.

670
00:43:47,160 --> 00:43:52,530
A queue that can't keep up just grows, and chapter seven shows what smeltr does then.

671
00:43:52,880 --> 00:43:58,360
So what does it mean when twice as many records are waiting, and they arrive no faster?

672
00:43:58,360 --> 00:44:00,500
Each one is waiting twice as long.

673
00:44:00,840 --> 00:44:03,140
So that's what healthy looks like.

674
00:44:03,140 --> 00:44:06,860
Both workers online at one commit, rows flowing on Map,

675
00:44:06,860 --> 00:44:11,590
fewer out than in because the noise is dropped, and nothing on Issues.

676
00:44:11,590 --> 00:44:13,890
Now, let's check your own numbers.

677
00:44:16,240 --> 00:44:17,460
Check on learning.

678
00:44:17,760 --> 00:44:22,130
In this feed, how do rows in and rows out compare?

679
00:44:27,480 --> 00:44:31,270
Fewer go out, because the pipeline drops the noise.

680
00:44:31,270 --> 00:44:33,490
Every login goes on to search.

681
00:44:35,360 --> 00:44:38,360
Where does smeltr list what needs your attention?

682
00:44:43,720 --> 00:44:44,420
On Issues.

683
00:44:44,420 --> 00:44:48,060
A red banner on every page shows when there are any.

684
00:44:52,560 --> 00:44:53,870
Chapter seven.

685
00:44:53,870 --> 00:44:55,270
When it breaks.

686
00:44:55,640 --> 00:44:56,760
Formats change.

687
00:44:56,760 --> 00:45:00,110
A new version of a program writes a new line,

688
00:45:00,110 --> 00:45:04,130
or an attacker does something your patterns never saw.

689
00:45:04,480 --> 00:45:07,420
What matters is that you hear about it.

690
00:45:07,420 --> 00:45:11,880
A line your pipeline can't read must say so, not disappear.

691
00:45:12,240 --> 00:45:13,680
Let's make it happen.

692
00:45:13,680 --> 00:45:18,260
On the server, try to log in as mallory, a user that doesn't exist.

693
00:45:18,260 --> 00:45:20,380
Guess a password, then give up.

694
00:45:20,760 --> 00:45:26,940
Mallory, by long tradition in security examples, is never up to anything good.

695
00:45:27,320 --> 00:45:32,460
sshd writes two lines your patterns don't know: Invalid user,

696
00:45:32,460 --> 00:45:35,580
and Failed password for invalid user.

697
00:45:35,960 --> 00:45:37,720
smeltr counts exceptions.

698
00:45:37,720 --> 00:45:42,710
On Map, the Steps tab of linux-logins shows them on the message parser.

699
00:45:46,880 --> 00:45:49,610
Each record carries its exceptions with it.

700
00:45:49,610 --> 00:45:51,260
Ask Elasticsearch for one.

701
00:45:55,880 --> 00:45:59,700
It shows the step that couldn't read the line, and why.

702
00:45:59,700 --> 00:46:03,580
The kind is Unparsable, and the message quotes the text.

703
00:46:03,920 --> 00:46:06,430
So the record still went on to search.

704
00:46:06,430 --> 00:46:10,320
smeltr tagged it with what went wrong, in smeltr.exception,

705
00:46:10,320 --> 00:46:12,700
and kept the line in event.original.

706
00:46:13,040 --> 00:46:14,220
Nothing was lost.

707
00:46:14,220 --> 00:46:20,450
But the record has no user and no source address, so a search for failed logins misses it.

708
00:46:20,760 --> 00:46:25,430
That's the practice for this chapter: bad data is loud, never silent.

709
00:46:25,430 --> 00:46:28,340
Loud means it's on the record, and counted.

710
00:46:28,340 --> 00:46:32,340
A dropped line would have hidden an attempt on your server.

711
00:46:32,640 --> 00:46:36,490
Tagging is smeltr's default answer for a line it can't parse.

712
00:46:36,490 --> 00:46:40,090
Open the route's Exceptions to see every answer it gives.

713
00:46:40,440 --> 00:46:46,620
The first four rows are categories of exception: parse, math, limits and encoding.

714
00:46:46,620 --> 00:46:51,440
The last row, Unmatched, is a record that no delivery rule took.

715
00:46:51,800 --> 00:46:57,680
Each column is an answer: ignore, tag, drop, deliver, or block.

716
00:46:58,560 --> 00:47:01,350
Choose by what a lost record costs you.

717
00:47:01,350 --> 00:47:05,140
For security logs, tag or deliver keeps the evidence.

718
00:47:05,140 --> 00:47:10,220
Drop throws it away, so choose drop only for data you truly don't need.

719
00:47:10,600 --> 00:47:11,390
Block waits.

720
00:47:11,390 --> 00:47:17,120
smeltr doesn't accept the record, and stops taking new ones until you fix the pipeline.

721
00:47:17,120 --> 00:47:20,880
For syslog, the sender's queue holds the lines meanwhile.

722
00:47:20,880 --> 00:47:24,300
Use it when a wrong record is worse than a late one.

723
00:47:24,680 --> 00:47:26,300
Fix it on a branch.

724
00:47:26,300 --> 00:47:28,100
Call it invalid-user.

725
00:47:36,080 --> 00:47:39,550
Capture the new lines, so the fix is checked against them.

726
00:48:08,240 --> 00:48:13,110
In the Failed pattern, make the words invalid user optional: parentheses,

727
00:48:13,110 --> 00:48:15,650
and a question mark, as in the header.

728
00:48:17,520 --> 00:48:20,900
And add a third pattern, for Invalid user.

729
00:48:23,760 --> 00:48:28,010
Its outcome comes out failure, because the line doesn't say Accepted.

730
00:48:29,920 --> 00:48:32,080
The test you saved still passes.

731
00:48:32,080 --> 00:48:34,640
The old lines read as they did before.

732
00:48:38,640 --> 00:48:41,960
Open it for review, read the change, and deploy it.

733
00:48:57,240 --> 00:48:58,960
Try mallory's login again.

734
00:49:08,440 --> 00:49:12,440
This time the records have their fields, and no exceptions.

735
00:49:19,360 --> 00:49:22,700
And the message parser counts no new exceptions.

736
00:49:30,080 --> 00:49:33,430
A line it can't read is one kind of break.

737
00:49:33,430 --> 00:49:36,790
Here's another: the destination goes away.

738
00:49:37,120 --> 00:49:41,100
Stop Elasticsearch, and log in on the server while it's down.

739
00:49:57,680 --> 00:50:01,700
Issues flags search: the destination is unreachable.

740
00:50:03,400 --> 00:50:05,620
It was working five minutes ago.

741
00:50:05,620 --> 00:50:08,050
Every on-call engineer has said it.

742
00:50:08,050 --> 00:50:09,920
Now you know where to look.

743
00:50:10,240 --> 00:50:13,940
smeltr holds the records it has read, and tries again.

744
00:50:13,940 --> 00:50:15,390
It doesn't drop them.

745
00:50:15,760 --> 00:50:21,270
If it stays down long enough, smeltr's buffer fills and the receiver stops reading.

746
00:50:21,270 --> 00:50:25,180
rsyslog then holds new lines in its queue: in memory first,

747
00:50:25,180 --> 00:50:28,040
and on disk once that nears its size limit.

748
00:50:28,360 --> 00:50:29,730
That's backpressure.

749
00:50:29,730 --> 00:50:34,870
Each part slows the one before it, instead of losing what it can't pass on.

750
00:50:35,200 --> 00:50:38,040
A log jam, in the most literal sense.

751
00:50:38,400 --> 00:50:40,260
Start Elasticsearch again.

752
00:50:41,160 --> 00:50:42,380
The issue clears.

753
00:50:42,380 --> 00:50:46,780
smeltr sends what it held, and every login reaches the index.

754
00:50:46,780 --> 00:50:48,000
Nothing was lost.

755
00:51:07,240 --> 00:51:11,580
In a longer outage, rsyslog's queue is what holds the lines.

756
00:51:11,580 --> 00:51:17,860
A sender with no queue, or one over UDP, loses what arrives while smeltr can't take it.

757
00:51:18,240 --> 00:51:21,810
smeltr held the records, and rsyslog queued them.

758
00:51:21,810 --> 00:51:23,700
So did every login arrive?

759
00:51:23,700 --> 00:51:26,900
Only the server and the index know for sure.

760
00:51:27,200 --> 00:51:29,390
That's the end-to-end argument.

761
00:51:29,390 --> 00:51:33,620
A guarantee only the two ends can check belongs at the ends.

762
00:51:33,620 --> 00:51:37,500
The parts between can help, but can't promise it alone.

763
00:51:37,500 --> 00:51:40,180
Saltzer, Reed and Clark made the case.

764
00:51:40,520 --> 00:51:42,780
So to check, compare the two ends.

765
00:51:42,780 --> 00:51:45,760
Count the logins the server wrote in an hour,

766
00:51:45,760 --> 00:51:48,750
and the logins the index holds for that hour.

767
00:51:48,750 --> 00:51:51,470
If the index holds fewer, some were lost.

768
00:51:51,840 --> 00:51:54,310
So that's smeltr when something breaks.

769
00:51:54,310 --> 00:51:59,000
A line it can't read is kept and counted, you fix the pattern on a branch,

770
00:51:59,000 --> 00:52:02,730
and the saved test shows the old lines still read the same.

771
00:52:02,730 --> 00:52:07,160
When the destination goes away, records wait instead of being dropped.

772
00:52:07,160 --> 00:52:09,000
And now, a check of your own.

773
00:52:11,320 --> 00:52:12,460
Check on learning.

774
00:52:12,840 --> 00:52:15,220
A line doesn't match any pattern.

775
00:52:15,220 --> 00:52:17,890
By default, where does the record go?

776
00:52:23,280 --> 00:52:27,630
On to its destination, with what went wrong in smeltr.exception,

777
00:52:27,630 --> 00:52:30,690
and the line as it arrived in event.original.

778
00:52:32,520 --> 00:52:33,880
You changed a pattern.

779
00:52:33,880 --> 00:52:36,980
How do you know the old lines still read the same?

780
00:52:42,320 --> 00:52:46,130
The test you saved runs on the sample, and still passes.

781
00:52:47,960 --> 00:52:49,910
Elasticsearch was down.

782
00:52:49,910 --> 00:52:51,770
Why was no login lost?

783
00:52:57,120 --> 00:53:01,800
smeltr held the records and tried again until Elasticsearch came back.

784
00:53:01,800 --> 00:53:05,210
In a longer outage, rsyslog's queue holds the rest.

785
00:53:07,120 --> 00:53:11,970
You've installed smeltr, brought in a real feed, shaped it, delivered it,

786
00:53:11,970 --> 00:53:13,760
and fixed it when it broke.

787
00:53:14,120 --> 00:53:16,230
That's an ore-some start.

788
00:53:16,230 --> 00:53:16,740
Sorry.

789
00:53:16,740 --> 00:53:18,590
It's a smelting thing.

790
00:53:18,920 --> 00:53:23,560
Test yourself on everything in this course with the self-test on the Learn page.

791
00:53:23,920 --> 00:53:27,310
When you're ready, the course for your role goes deeper.

